{"id": 3, "ts": 1780270916.1755965, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I\u2019d like to start the discussion by putting forward Candidate\u202fB as my provisional recommendation.\u202fFrom my notes, Candidate\u202fB is described as \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d which directly supports operational reliability, and \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d addressing the critical need for calmness under pressure on long\u2011distance flights. Additionally, B\u2019s ability to assess weather conditions and strong computer skills indicate solid technical competence for complex flight planning and systems management.\nThe main reservation I have is the reported tendency to make \u201cnasty remarks about his colleagues,\u201d which raises concerns about crew cooperation and professional communication. However, the strengths in reliability, stress resilience, and technical ability are foundational for safety\u2011critical operations, and I believe they outweigh the interpersonal drawback, especially if we can address it through targeted onboarding and team\u2011building measures.", "thoughts": "We need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON. No prior discussion, so we need to start. We must make a focused argumentative contribution, stating current recommendation, referencing discussion (none), using explicit facts, explain implication.\n\nWe have candidate facts. Need to choose a candidate. Let's evaluate quickly.\n\nCriteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate A: can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. So strengths: anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership. Concerns: show-off (maybe cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). No explicit stress resilience